WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
Where do the neurons of the cerebral cortex come from?

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.320
In rodents, most come from one kind of cell.

3
00:00:10.283 --> 00:00:13.683
Here is a slice through the wall of the embryonic mouse cortex.

4
00:00:14.563 --> 00:00:17.243
The ventricle, a fluid-filled space, is at the bottom.

5
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:20.803
The surface facing the ventricle is apical.

6
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:23.903
The outer surface, under the pia, is basal.

7
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:29.137
Beside the ventricle lies the ventricular zone, where progenitors live.

8
00:00:29.977 --> 00:00:32.137
A progenitor is a cell that still divides.

9
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.990
Just outside it is the subventricular zone.

10
00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:41.170
Farther out, young neurons cross the intermediate zone to reach the cortical plate.

11
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:43.513
Now light up one cell.

12
00:00:44.353 --> 00:00:45.373
It spans the whole wall.

13
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:48.593
An apical foot sits on the ventricle.

14
00:00:49.293 --> 00:00:52.073
A long fibre ends in a basal foot under the pia.

15
00:00:53.117 --> 00:00:56.477
Early on, cells like this are neuroepithelial cells.

16
00:00:57.337 --> 00:01:03.137
As neurogenesis begins, in mouse around embryonic days 10 to 12, they become radial

17
00:01:03.137 --> 00:01:04.137
glia.

18
00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:05.850
They keep the long shape.

19
00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.150
And they switch on proteins associated with glia.

20
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:11.453
Look where cells divide.

21
00:01:12.453 --> 00:01:15.533
Most cells caught in mitosis sit against the ventricle.

22
00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:18.373
Cells copying their DNA sit farther out.

23
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:20.617
Two words will matter.

24
00:01:21.457 --> 00:01:24.777
A stem cell renews itself and makes more than one kind of cell.

25
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.990
Radial glia, as a population, meet both conditions.

26
00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:33.050
An asymmetric division keeps one radial glial cell.

27
00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:37.090
Some single lineages later make glia as well as neurons.

28
00:01:38.183 --> 00:01:39.823
And potential is not fate.

29
00:01:41.123 --> 00:01:43.243
Potential is what a cell can make alone in a dish.

30
00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:45.863
Fate is what it does make in the embryo.

31
00:01:46.983 --> 00:01:48.223
Now the first puzzle.

32
00:01:49.123 --> 00:01:52.923
In fetal monkey cortex, young neurons climb radial glial fibres.

33
00:01:53.943 --> 00:01:56.023
Is the scaffold also the source of the neurons?

34
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:58.183
The second puzzle.

35
00:01:59.143 --> 00:02:04.083
Why does a progenitor copy its DNA out in the ventricular zone, then come back to

36
00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:05.083
the ventricle to divide?

37
00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.047
Here is the idea to carry through.

38
00:02:08.047 --> 00:02:12.407
A radial glial cell is a stem cell anchored at the ventricle.

39
00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:17.127
Each time it divides, it decides how many daughters stay radial glia.

40
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:20.640
Keep two, and its radial glial line doubles.

41
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.300
Keep one, and the line holds steady while it makes neurons.

42
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.480
Keep none, and its radial glial line ends.

43
00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:31.003
A daughter that leaves is spent as a stem cell.

44
00:02:31.823 --> 00:02:35.983
It becomes a neuron, or an intermediate progenitor that usually makes two.

45
00:02:36.967 --> 00:02:41.067
Count those choices round by round, and you can see how the number of neurons

46
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:42.067
depends on them.

47
00:02:43.967 --> 00:02:48.407
This is a counting model, for projection neurons of rodent dorsal cortex.

48
00:02:49.487 --> 00:02:53.527
Outer radial glia, common in primates, renew themselves without the anchor.

49
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.720
And the rule counts.

50
00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.240
It does not explain what makes a daughter stay or leave.

51
00:03:00.283 --> 00:03:01.963
So hold on to one picture.

52
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:07.003
One long cell across the wall, spending its divisions one choice at a time.
